<Item XName="Note" UserName="Note" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;DIV style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 1pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in; mso-element: para-border-div; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .75pt&quot;&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: medium none; PADDING-BOTTOM: 0in; TEXT-ALIGN: center; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .75pt; mso-padding-alt: 0in 0in 1.0pt 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 18pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Radar Frequency Modulation Signal Example&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies SystemVue Example Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;u1&quot; /&gt;&lt;u1:p&gt;&lt;/u1:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;u1:p&gt;************************************************&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;Simulation Task&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;************************************************&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;This workspace shows how to generate several&amp;nbsp;forms of frequency modulated signals, including FSK signal, Costas Frequency Hopping signal, Hybrid FSK/PSK signal, and nonlinear FM signal.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;************************************************&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;Workspace Description&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;************************************************&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%&apos;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The Linear Frequency Modulation (LFM) is NOT the only frequency modulation law used in radar. For LFM the volume of the ambiguity function was concentrated in a slowly decaying diagonal ridge, and the LFM suffered from high autocorrelation sidelobes, therefore some form of amplitude weighting was necessary in order to reshape the spectrum and thus reduce those sidelobes. &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;This example introduces the various other kinds of frequency modulation, including Costas frequency coding, FSK, Hybrid FSK/PSK and Nonlinear FM as complementary demonstrations. The 1&lt;SUP&gt;st&lt;/SUP&gt; three types belong to the FSK category, and the 2&lt;SUP&gt;nd&lt;/SUP&gt; is shown in a separate folder.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;1, FSK Examples &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;1) Costas Code Example&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The Costas Code is actually a discrete frequency coding that is practically the opposite of the LFM linear law. The hoppling order strongly affects the ambiguity function of the signal. The uniqueness for a Costas signal is that the number of coinciding dots cannot be larger than one for all but the zero-shift case, and this implies a narrow peak of the ACF at the origin and low sidelobes elsewhere.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;This Costas frequency coding example shows how to generate the Costas signal with the frequency sequence fj = {4, 7, 1, 6, 5, 2, 3} * 1e5 Hz. The ACF, time-domain complex waveform and power spectrum are shown.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;2) FSK Example&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;In an FSK waveform, the transmitted frequency fj is chosen from the Frequency Hopping (FH) sequence {f1, f2, . . . , fNF } of available frequencies for transmission at a set of consecutive time intervals {t1, t2, . . . , tNF }. The frequencies are placed in the various time slots corresponding to a binary time-frequency matrix. Each frequency is used once within the code period, with one frequency per time slot and one time slot per frequency. &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;This FSK example shows&amp;nbsp;how to generate&amp;nbsp;FSK signal with FHSequence = [1e6, 2e6, 3e6] Hz and TimeIntervals = [10e-6, 10e-6, 10e-6]s, for which each time interval is 1/10 of PRI. The &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;time-domain complex waveform and power spectrum are shown.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;3) Hybrid FSK/PSK Example&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;The hybrid FSK/PSK example demonstrates how to generate the hybrid FSK and PSK signal with one of the typical Costas frequency sequence = [2,4,8,5,10,9,7,3,6,1]*2e5 (Delta_Frequency) and the PSK code is Barker code with the length of 13. &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;The &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;time-domain complex waveform and power spectrum are shown.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;mso-spacerun: yes&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;2, Nonlinear FM Example &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;As mentioned earlier, the linear frequency modulation waveform suffers from high sidelobes in the ACF curves, and some form of amplitude weighting is a must. However, this variable amplitude transmission requires linear power amplifiers, which are less efficient than saturated power amplifiers. This drawback naturally leads to the design of nonlinear FM, to deviate from the constant rate of frequency change and spend more time at frequencies that need to be enhanced.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;This&amp;nbsp;workspace shows how to generate Non-Linear Frequency Modulation Waveform signal with&amp;nbsp; Radar NLFM model, and the corresponding results from the LFM model are provided for comparison. &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;As it is difficult to distinguish NLFM and LFM signal in the time domain, the Power Spectrum, Frequency-Time Graph and Chirp Rate (with simple post simulation processing scripts) comparisons between NLFM and LFM signal are proposed. The blue curve represents NLFM signal and the red one is LFM signal. &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 13.5pt; LINE-HEIGHT: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;Compared with LFM waveform, NLFM frequency ramp is non-linear, with steeper slope at the beginning and at the end of the pulse. Thus, chirp rate pattern of NLFM is like &quot;U&quot; shape.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; mso-layout-grid-align: none&quot;&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;I&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; COLOR: black; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt&apos;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;References: &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/I&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; mso-layout-grid-align: none&quot;&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;I&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; COLOR: black; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt&apos;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;[1] Nadav Levanon and Eli Mozeson, &amp;lt;Radar Signals&amp;gt;, 2004, John Wiley &amp;amp; Sons Inc.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/I&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; mso-layout-grid-align: none&quot;&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;I&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; COLOR: black; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt&apos;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;[2] Phillip E. Pace,&amp;nbsp;&amp;lt;Detecting and Classifying Low Probability of Intercept Radar&amp;gt;, 2009, ARTECH HOUSE, MA.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/I&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; mso-layout-grid-align: none&quot;&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;I&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; COLOR: black; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt&apos;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;[3] Mark Richards,&amp;nbsp;&amp;lt;Fundamentals of Radar Signal Processing&amp;gt;, 2005, MCGraw-Hill, New York.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/I&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; LINE-HEIGHT: normal; mso-layout-grid-align: none&quot;&gt;&lt;B&gt;&lt;I&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-SIZE: 12pt; COLOR: black; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; mso-ascii-font-family: Calibri; mso-hansi-font-family: Calibri; mso-font-kerning: 12.0pt&apos;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;[4] Merrill I. Skolnik,&amp;nbsp;&amp;lt;Introduction to Radar Systems, Third Edition&amp;gt;, 2001, MCGraw-Hill, New York.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/I&gt;&lt;/B&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/u1:p&gt;</Text>
